===README SECURITY===
## Security posture and known gaps
- **CORS:** none, on purpose. This is a server-to-server API. A browser client would sit behind a same-origin gateway or get an explicit allow-list.
- **Authentication:** static seeded bearer tokens (dev only), stored as SHA-256 hashes. There is no expiry, rotation or signup. Production would use OIDC or JWT.
- **Tenant isolation:** enforced in SQL (membership joins). It is tested by byte-identical 404s and by a fuzzer that checks for cross-team leaks under load.
- **SQL injection:** every backend uses parameterized statements. An audit of string-built SQL found only constant fragments (column lists, `deleted_at IS [NOT] NULL`). Python's PATCH builds `SET` from field names that the validator has already restricted to `title` and `body`; an allowlist in the store layer would add defense in depth.
- **Input limits:** 2 MiB bodies (413), I-JSON only, 200 and 100,000 code-point limits.
- **Not yet done:**
  - pagination on lists (the `seq` column is already in place for keyset paging)
  - rate limiting
  - `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff` and other security headers
  - dependency audits (`npm audit`, `cargo audit`)
  - non-root Docker users
- **Dev servers:** the Python and PHP backends run on development HTTP servers. Grok's red team found request-framing weaknesses there (conflicting Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding) and a ~30 µs timing difference for hidden notes on Deno and PHP ([`grok/redteam-findings.md`](https://github.com/CoeusCC/team-notes-polyglot/blob/grok/workspace/grok/redteam-findings.md)).

## What I'd change, add, or stop doing with more time
===INVARIANTS HEAD===
#!/usr/bin/env node
// Stateful property-based fuzzer for the Team Notes REST service. Zero deps, seeded PRNG.
//   node redteam/invariants.mjs --target node-ts=http://127.0.0.1:3201 [--target ...] --seed 1,2 --ops 1500 [--out evidence/redteam.invariants]
// Invariants (see redteam/README.md): I1 no 5xx, I2 problem+json w/ matching status, I3 no cross-team leak,
// I4 monotonic versions / rejected PATCH leaves state alone, I5 no lost update (+ 16-way burst), I6 deleted semantics,
// I7 ETag == "<version>", I8 unauthenticated never 2xx except /health. Plus M: model/oracle agreement (status codes per ADR-0003 precedence).
import http from 'node:http';
import { writeFileSync } from 'node:fs';

const argv = process.argv.slice(2);
const targets = []; let seeds = [1], OPS = 1000, out = null;
for (let i = 0; i < argv.length; i++) {
  if (argv[i] === '--target') { const [n, ...u] = argv[++i].split('='); targets.push({ name: n, url: u.join('=') }); }
  else if (argv[i] === '--seed') seeds = argv[++i].split(',').map(Number);
  else if (argv[i] === '--ops') OPS = Number(argv[++i]);
  else if (argv[i] === '--out') out = argv[++i];
}
if (!targets.length) { console.error('need --target name=url'); process.exit(2); }

// ---- seeded PRNG (mulberry32) ----
function rng(seed) { let a = seed >>> 0; const f = () => { a = (a + 0x6D2B79F5) >>> 0; let t = a; t = Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 15), t | 1); t ^= t + Math.imul(t ^ (t >>> 7), t | 61); return ((t ^ (t >>> 14)) >>> 0) / 4294967296; };
  f.int = (n) => Math.floor(f() * n); f.pick = (a) => a[f.int(a.length)]; f.chance = (p) => f() < p;
  f.weighted = (pairs) => { const tot = pairs.reduce((s, p) => s + p[1], 0); let r = f() * tot; for (const [v, w] of pairs) { if ((r -= w) < 0) return v; } return pairs[0][0]; };
  return f; }

const TOK = { alice: 'tok_alice', bob: 'tok_bob', carol: 'tok_carol', none: null, bad: 'nope' };
const TEAM_OF = { alice: 'team-red', bob: 'team-red', carol: 'team-blue' };
const INVS = ['I1', 'I2', 'I3', 'I4', 'I5', 'I6', 'I7', 'I8', 'M'];

function request(base, method, path, { token, headers = {}, body, timeout = 15000 } = {}) {
  return new Promise((resolve) => {
    const u = new URL(base);
    const h = { ...headers };
    if (token) h.authorization = `Bearer ${token}`;
    let buf = null;
    if (body !== undefined) { buf = Buffer.from(body, 'utf8'); h['content-length'] = buf.length; }
    let done = false; const fin = (v) => { if (!done) { done = true; resolve(v); } };
    const efin = (e) => setTimeout(() => fin({ error: String(e) }), 150); // a response may still be in flight (early 413 + reset)
    const req = http.request({ host: u.hostname, port: u.port, method, path, headers: h, agent: false, timeout }, (res) => {
      const ch = []; res.on('data', (c) => ch.push(c));
      res.on('end', () => { const text = Buffer.concat(ch).toString('utf8'); let json; try { json = JSON.parse(text); } catch { /* */ }
        fin({ status: res.statusCode, headers: res.headers, text, json }); });
      res.on('error', efin);
    });
    req.on('timeout', () => { req.destroy(); fin({ error: 'timeout' }); });
    req.on('error', efin);
    if (buf) req.write(buf); req.end();
  });
}

const HOSTILE_STR = ['', ' ', '   ', '\t\n', '\u0000', 'a\u0000b', '😀', '日本語', '‮', '\ud800', 'é'.normalize('NFD'), "'; DROP TABLE notes;--", '<script>x</script>', '../../etc/passwd', '%00', '__proto__', 'constructor', '{"a":1}'];
const WRONG = [null, 1, 1.5, true, false, [], {}, [1], { a: 1 }, '', 'x'.repeat(201), 12345678901234567890];

async function runOne(target, seed, OPS) {
  const R = rng(seed), base = target.url;
  const checks = Object.fromEntries(INVS.map((i) => [i, 0]));
  const viol = []; const vcount = Object.fromEntries(INVS.map((i) => [i, 0]));
  const trace = []; // {n, desc, noteIdx}
  const notes = []; // model: {id, team, version, deleted, title, body}
  const redIds = new Set();
===REDTEAM README===
# Red team

Goal: not "the service is perfect", but "under stated conditions it cannot fail in these specific ways", plus evidence that the tests can detect defects.

## Threat model
Attacker is any HTTP client on the wire: unauthenticated, bad token, a legitimate user of the other team (carol vs team-red), or a teammate racing another teammate. Capabilities: arbitrary paths, methods, headers (`If-Match`, `Content-Type`, `Authorization`), bodies (unicode, huge, wrong types, extra/read-only fields, `__proto__`), and parallel requests. Out of scope: network-level attacks, TLS, token theft/guessing, DoS/performance, anything beyond static seeded tokens.

## Invariants ("cannot fail in these ways")
Conditions: one server process, one SQLite file, seeded users (alice/bob on team-red, carol on team-blue), HTTP/1.1, fresh DB.
- **I1** no 5xx for any request sequence.
- **I2** every 4xx is `application/problem+json` with `status` equal to the HTTP status.
- **I3** carol never gets 2xx on a team-red path and never sees a team-red note id in any response body.
- **I4** a note's version never decreases; every 200 PATCH is +1; a 412/428 PATCH leaves stored state untouched (re-GET). Restore of a deleted note is +1, of a live note is +0.
- **I5** no lost update: a PATCH whose If-Match is stale, future, weak, `*`, missing or garbage is never applied. Under concurrency, 16 parallel PATCHes with the same If-Match yield exactly one 200 and fifteen 412, and the final version is v+1.
- **I6** deleted notes are never returned by GET or the live list, and always appear in `?archived=true` for team members until restored.
- **I7** `ETag == "<body.version>"` on every note response.
- **I8** no 2xx without a valid token, except `/health`.
- **M** (model agreement): expected status per ADR-0003 precedence (401, 404, 415, 413, 400/422, 428, 412), trimmed titles, list contents versus the fuzzer's own model.

"Checked" means evaluated after every response in a random sequence (seeded mulberry32 PRNG; the seed and request lines are printed) plus a deterministic probe sweep of ~40 hostile paths x 6 methods x 3 callers per run. This is evidence by search, not a proof: absence of violations over N ops is not exhaustive.

## Run
```
redteam/run-all.sh 1,2 1500            # starts all 5 backends (ports 3201-3206, fresh DB, own process group), fuzzes, stops them
redteam/run-all.sh 1,2 1500 python     # one backend
node redteam/invariants.mjs --target node-ts=http://127.0.0.1:3201 --seed 7 --ops 2000   # against any running server
```
Output: `evidence/redteam.invariants.{json,md}`. Exit 1 if any violation. The "sequence touching the note" printed per violation is the slice of the log that touched that note (a causal slice, not an automatically delta-debugged minimum). Repro = same seed against a fresh server.

Mutation testing: see `evidence/redteam.mutation.md` (Uncle Bob's `mutator`, run on scratch copies).

## Judgement calls built into the oracle
Behaviours the spec does not pin are treated as "invariants only": duplicate or case-variant `archived` query params, `?archived=` (empty), an If-Match list that contains the current ETag, duplicate JSON keys, lone surrogates, `HEAD`/`OPTIONS`, junk team ids in the URL. Observed divergence between backends on these is reported, not failed.

## Not proven
- Performance or behaviour under real network conditions (slow clients, resets, keep-alive reuse, proxies, HTTP/2). Only loopback HTTP/1.1, one connection per request.
- Multi-process or multi-host SQLite on every runtime (the burst test is 16 in-flight requests to a single server process; PHP's built-in server and the others were not tested with several workers on one file).
- Auth beyond the three static tokens: no token rotation, expiry, membership changes (Tier 3), or timing side channels.
- Tier 3 (teams, tags, search, paging) is not modelled.
- The mutation run covers only the pure domain cores with unit tests, not `store`/`http` layers or the Rust/PHP/Deno backends.

